module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.v2_lexer_carries_no_layout_so_a_newline_before_a_dual_role_operator_merges_silently

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data v2_lexer_carries_no_layout_so_a_newline_before_a_dual_role_operator_merges_silently: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "v2_lexer_carries_no_layout_so_a_newline_before_a_dual_role_operator_merges_silently" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a statement followed by a line that begins with an operator that is both prefix and infix (`-`) is read by v2 as ONE binary expression, where the seed refuses it as ambiguous** (INVALID STATE: `x` on one line and `-1` on the next, or `f(a)` then `-b`, parse in v2 as `x - 1` / `f(a) - b`. The second statement is gone, absorbed as a right operand, and nothing is reported.) The seed refuses the same source with 'ambiguous newline before prefix/infix operator '-': move the infix operator before the newline or parenthesize the unary expression', so the two parsers disagree on the same text, and the v2 reading is silently different code.",
    "WHERE, cited by symbol. The seed decides it with layout: `v1.compiler.parse` `is_ambiguous_prefix_infix_newline_boundary` fires when a NEWLINE token precedes an operator in `operator_continuation_dual_role_excluded_symbols` (today exactly `-`). v2 has no such fact to decide with. `v2.std.compilers.lexing` `Token` carries `class`, `lexeme`, `file`, `start` and `end` and nothing about the layout between tokens, and the dag lex rules emit no newline token, so no grammar production can distinguish `x - 1` from `x` newline `-1`. The gap is in the LEXER MODEL, not in a production: a parser special case would be a second, positional authority for a fact the token stream does not carry.",
    "SCOPE. Only an operator that can also START an expression can open a statement and be absorbed; today that is `-`. An operator that is infix only (`*`, `&&`, `|>`, `==`) cannot begin a statement, so a line starting with it never parsed as a separate statement in either parser and is not this class. The population grows with every construct v2 reads as a complete operand: a block-headed statement (`match w {..}` then a `-` line) joins it once block forms are operands of binary_expr, which is why the block-headed left-operand repair (quiet-gull-780's grammar lane) waits on this row's trigger rather than landing a merge the seed refuses.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor (silent wrongness): the parse accepts and the lowered value computes a different expression. CEILING: structurally guaranteed. The ambiguity is decidable from the source text, so it can refuse with a typed, located diagnostic exactly where the seed does. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: the v2 token stream carries layout (a newline boundary between two tokens is a fact the grammar can read), and the dag grammar refuses a dual-role operator that follows a newline at an operand boundary with a typed reason from the closed `v2.std.parse_refusal_reason` vocabulary, as the seed refuses it -- for every statement form, not only block-headed ones.",
    "CLIMBED, THE TRIGGER CAPABILITY MET (gunbc#12773, gunbc#12881). The token stream carries layout: `v2.std.compilers.lexing` `TokenStream` carries a `StreamLayout` recorded in the tokenizer's own walk (`v2.compiler.tokenize` `lex_walk_step`), and `token_stream_line_break_before` answers whether a line break precedes a token by lookup; a stream with no layout answers unknown and a layout-conditioned production refuses `LayoutUnknownWithoutSource` rather than assuming one line. The grammar refuses: `v2.extdeps.languages.dag` `dag_grammar_additive_expr_helper` tries `RefuseOnMatch { AfterLineBreak { - }, NewlineBeforeDualRoleOperator }` first at every additive continuation, so a `-` that begins a line after a complete operand refuses at the `-` with that typed `v2.std.parse_refusal_reason` reason, for every statement form, block-headed ones included; a same-line `-` is subtraction. RUNG NOW: structurally guaranteed -- no Accepted program contains a line-break-preceded dual-role operator at an operand boundary. The class stays rostered: the defect is real and the evidence below is what keeps the rung honest (DESIGN 4b(4)).",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.parse.newline_dual_role_operator_parse", decl_name: "a_newline_minus_after_an_operand_refuses_holds", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.parse.newline_dual_role_operator_parse", decl_name: "a_newline_minus_after_a_block_refuses_holds", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.parse.newline_dual_role_operator_parse", decl_name: "a_same_line_minus_is_subtraction_holds", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.parse_test_newline_dual_role_operator_mutation", decl_name: "witness_the_live_grammar_carries_the_newline_refusal", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.parse_test_newline_dual_role_operator_mutation", decl_name: "witness_without_the_refusal_a_next_line_minus_after_an_operand_merges_RED", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.parse_test_newline_dual_role_operator_mutation", decl_name: "witness_without_the_refusal_a_next_line_minus_after_a_block_merges_RED", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
